1
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:03,116
Does that sort of crime,
in this case murder,

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,599
have a name?

3
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:06,193
It's called a sadistic murder.

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:08,920
How do your studies
describe such a killer?

5
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,799
It's usually someone
who suffered emotional trauma

6
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:15,191
followed by long emotional isolation.

7
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,316
- Is there a purpose to the crime?
- Revenge.

8
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:12,988
Am I you, or are you me?

9
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:15,718
- Hurry up.
- Okay, okay.

10
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:17,199
Do you have my notebook?

11
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:20,272
- Emily, let's go. You don't need it.
- Okay.

12
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,590
Julia, can't we do this another night?

13
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:24,955
Emily, we should've been at the library
ten minutes ago.

14
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:27,270
- Let's go.
- Julia.

15
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:31,274
Come on. You like playing with fire.

16
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:47,028
- Good night, Aunt Esther.
- Good night.

17
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,954
Don't you girls believe
in having a little fun now and then?

18
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:54,438
- This is fun.
- Studying day and night?

19
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,637
Actually,
we're working on a project right now.

20
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:00,473
Abnormal psychology.

21
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:04,237
- Well, have a nice time.
- How's the book?

22
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:06,991
Incredibly great.

23
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:09,720
This poor girl
has just been swept off her feet

24
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:14,351
and ravished by the highwayman
who killed her father in a duel.

25
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:17,193
Very romantic.

26
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:19,954
- Aunt Esther, l--
- We have to go.

27
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,792
Bye.

28
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:28,079
Goodbye.

29
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:36,197
<i>Back to Grimley,</i>
<i>back to the 35-yard line.</i>

30
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:37,634
<i>He passes the ball down the left.</i>

31
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,552
<i>It's complete to Elwes,</i>
<i>ladies and gentlemen.</i>

32
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:42,678
- Good night, Uncle Harold.
- Good night.

33
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:02,470
Good evening, Miss Reese.

34
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,150
Thank you, Mrs. Reese.

35
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:39,112
<i>Out of bounds at the 50-yard line.</i>
<i>It's the first--</i>

36
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:33,235
Harold? Is that you?

37
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:45,278
Harold?

38
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:00,437
Harold?

39
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:06,392
- Julia.
- Sorry.

40
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:39,319
Yes?

41
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:42,038
Very well, Emily.

42
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:46,671
- That work is in French, you know.
- Yes, ma'am.

43
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:52,668
<i>Lined up for the kick-off.</i>

44
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:56,518
<i>Johnson is getting ready to kick.</i>

45
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:58,878
<i>Referee gives the whistle.</i>

46
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:02,791
<i>There's the kick.</i>
<i>It's deep into the end zone.</i>

47
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,188
<i>And Wilson has got the ball.</i>

48
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:06,749
<i>He's bringing it out,</i>
<i>ladies and gentlemen.</i>

49
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:08,319
Hey. Hey!

50
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:15,432
<i>Heading for the 25- 35-</i>
<i>45-yard line.</i>

51
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:36,668
Esther? Esther, are you all ri--?

52
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,515
Now, you say you came home
at what time, Mr. Henderson?

53
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:47,999
About a half an hour ago.

54
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:50,754
I got here just before the cop--

55
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:53,036
I mean, you boys, got here.

56
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:55,714
And I can prove it. I got witnesses.

57
00:08:55,880 --> 00:08:57,552
Why don't you get yourself
a cup of coffee,

58
00:08:57,720 --> 00:08:59,438
and we'll talk some more?

59
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:02,354
Lieutenant,
found these boots around back.

60
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:05,080
They fit the prints in the flowerbed.

61
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:09,113
Thanks.

62
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:10,674
Mr. Scully.

63
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:15,997
Have you ever
seen these boots before, sir?

64
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:17,149
Yes, they're mine.

65
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:19,754
I keep them out in the shed
for gardening.

66
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:22,559
- But what has that got to do with--?
- They're yours, you're sure?

67
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:24,915
Well, of course I'm sure. But why?

68
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:30,278
Mr. Scully, can you explain

69
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,635
why the glass is broken
from the inside?

70
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:35,914
The inside?

71
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:37,433
And the intruder,

72
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:39,431
why would he
be wearing your boots, sir?

73
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:41,272
Uncle Harold?

74
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:44,068
What happened?

75
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:49,830
Esther is dead.

76
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,434
She's been murdered.

77
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:07,517
- Well, good morning.
- It's morning, all right.

78
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:09,875
As to the good part,
I'll tell you after I wake up.

79
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:11,758
Bad night?

80
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:14,559
Irving Turley's dog barked all night.

81
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:17,553
Then my hot-water heater went out
and I had to take a cold shower.

82
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:18,914
Then the car wouldn't start.

83
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:21,753
Well, why don't you talk to Turley
about that dog?

84
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:24,878
I would have, but I had to wake him up
to push the car.

85
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:26,155
What's the emergency?

86
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:28,390
Well, I couldn't find Charlene,
so I came down.

87
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:30,790
- You couldn't find Charlene?
- No.

88
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:32,075
Where was she?

89
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:35,232
I don't know.
That's why I couldn't find her.

90
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:38,119
- Well, who's the client?
- Harold Scully.

91
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:42,590
Harold Scully?
I'm going back to bed.

92
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:43,954
Ben, it's a homicide.

93
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:47,751
A homicide?
Harold Scully couldn't kill anybody.

94
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:49,911
He might bore a few people to death.

95
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:52,310
It's his wife. It happened last night.

96
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:54,759
Harold Scully?

97
00:10:58,600 --> 00:10:59,794
I don't know, Frank.

98
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,678
I think you've got enough
to question him,

99
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:03,592
but I think
you're getting ahead of yourself.

100
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:06,752
- Well, how is that, Ben?
- What's your evidence?

101
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:08,194
Got it right here.

102
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:11,750
The boys found it this morning,
hidden in the basement.

103
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:13,069
How's it test?

104
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:16,038
It's all there. Hair, blood.

105
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:19,158
Now, the killer tried to clean it off,
but he botched it.

106
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:23,593
Wanna see your client now?

107
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:26,630
I'll have to discuss it
with my advisor.

108
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,513
Excuse me.

109
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:40,228
- They're gonna charge him.
- He's in the interview room.

110
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:43,470
I can't stand Harold Scully.

111
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:46,595
I used to like to play golf at Lakeside.

112
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:49,513
Somehow Harold always wound up
in the foursome.

113
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:51,750
He'd tell me how to stand,
how to look at the ball,

114
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:53,114
how to swing at it.

115
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:54,838
Half the time it'd go in the woods.

116
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,231
He'd run and find it
and point to it like a Labrador retriever.

117
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:00,795
Tell him I'm busy.

118
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:03,428
- You tell him you're busy.
- Tell him I moved to Philadelphia.

119
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:06,194
You tell him
you moved to Philadelphia.

120
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:09,879
You're a hard case, Tyler.

121
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:27,671
Harold,
I'm terribly sorry about Esther.

122
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:31,230
- Terribly sorry.
- Ben.

123
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:33,673
Thank you for coming, Ben.

124
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:35,831
Why would anyone
wanna kill her, Ben?

125
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,878
Why? Why?

126
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:42,317
Harold, you're gonna
have to turn me loose.

127
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:47,231
Sit down, Harold.

128
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:50,631
Ben, they think I did it.

129
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:52,995
Can you believe that?

130
00:12:53,680 --> 00:12:55,955
Harold, let go of my hand.

131
00:12:56,720 --> 00:13:01,396
Now, it's true
they are gonna arrest you.

132
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:04,795
I loved her, Ben.

133
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:08,238
I loved her. Why would I kill her?

134
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:12,712
Harold, you're gonna have to sit
by yourself.

135
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:15,713
Now, there's something
I wanna say to you.

136
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:18,838
Let me just say something
to you first.

137
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,070
I've always looked up to you.

138
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,835
I know I'm just some guy
who married a rich woman.

139
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,309
And I know
you've always thought I was a bore.

140
00:13:27,480 --> 00:13:31,155
I am. I bore myself.

141
00:13:31,320 --> 00:13:34,835
And I know you've avoided me.
I don't blame you.

142
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,232
I know I've made a pest out of myself
trying to get you to like me.

143
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:44,435
I miss our golf.

144
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:48,272
I don't know why I'm going on
about this.

145
00:13:48,440 --> 00:13:50,158
I guess I should just say,

146
00:13:50,320 --> 00:13:53,153
thank you for taking my case, Ben.

147
00:13:53,720 --> 00:13:54,835
Thank you.

148
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:00,716
- Harold?
- Yes, Ben?

149
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:07,990
- I'll go see about bail.
- Thank you, Ben.

150
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:34,230
- There he is.
- Where is he?

151
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:35,435
- Over there.
- Yes.

152
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,673
Well, we got a locked house
and a locked shed.

153
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:45,829
If Harold didn't do it,

154
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,672
then somebody with keys
to both places did.

155
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,956
- The gardener lives above the garage.
- Is he there?

156
00:14:50,120 --> 00:14:52,270
If he is, he's not answering.

157
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:55,557
Well, Harold says
that he's got keys to both places.

158
00:14:55,720 --> 00:14:58,712
The gardener says that he was
at a place called Archie's Tavern

159
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:00,074
during the murder.

160
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:02,549
- Wanna see if he was?
- Okay.

161
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:04,836
How are you gonna plead him?

162
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,468
I don't know. Poor Harold.

163
00:15:07,640 --> 00:15:10,712
I just don't think he did this.
He hadn't exercised since 1 948.

164
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:12,996
I don't think he's strong enough
to kill Esther.

165
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:15,594
Look, he had motive
and he had access.

166
00:15:15,760 --> 00:15:19,036
He is the sole beneficiary
to Esther's will.

167
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:21,031
I don't believe
that masked-intruder story

168
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:22,633
for a heartbeat, do you?

169
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:24,836
- I don't know.
- And his boots.

170
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,197
His boots are the ones
that made the footprints.

171
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:30,954
You keep trying
to make me feel better, don't you?

172
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:33,632
There she is.

173
00:15:35,320 --> 00:15:37,993
- Good morning.
- Morning.

174
00:15:38,160 --> 00:15:40,310
People have been looking for you
all morning.

175
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:42,994
Were people concerned
or just curious, Dad?

176
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:45,118
Goodbye.

177
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:47,191
- We do have a case, you know.
- Yeah.

178
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:49,510
I've done a complete interview
with Harold Scully.

179
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:51,432
I booked him into a hotel
away from the press.

180
00:15:51,600 --> 00:15:54,068
Oh, there's a couple who live in,
but they're away on vacation.

181
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:55,229
Anything else?

182
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:59,109
What about the sisters watching us
from the window up there?

183
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:06,677
Let's see.
''Parents killed when they were young.

184
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:09,354
Boarding schools all their lives.
Living here while at college.

185
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:12,273
Big inheritance from their father.
Brilliant students.''

186
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:15,998
- Where were they during the murder?
- They were at the library, studying.

187
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:18,116
- Do you want me to check it out?
- Yeah.

188
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:20,430
Will you be somewhere
where I <i>can..?</i>

189
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:23,238
I'll be around.

190
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:54,513
- Mrs. Reese?
- Yes?

191
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,238
I'm Charlene Matlock.

192
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:58,595
I'm an attorney
representing Harold Scully,

193
00:16:58,760 --> 00:17:00,512
and I wanted
to ask you a couple of questions

194
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:02,477
about Emily and Julia McCullough.

195
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:04,392
What about them?

196
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:08,599
Well, now, were they both here
the night of the murder?

197
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:12,196
They came in at 8 and left at 1 0,
when the library closed.

198
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:14,794
And they were both here
the whole time?

199
00:17:15,400 --> 00:17:19,154
- What are you suggesting?
- I'm not suggesting anything.

200
00:17:19,320 --> 00:17:21,914
Well, let me put your mind at rest.

201
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:24,994
Shortly after 9:00
that particular evening,

202
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:27,196
Emily asked me
for a reference book.

203
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:29,874
And a few minutes before that,

204
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:33,555
I saw Julia up there, in the stacks.

205
00:17:35,040 --> 00:17:38,999
- You're very precise.
- I suppose that is unusual.

206
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:43,549
Yeah. Well, thanks, Mrs. Reese.

207
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:11,993
Hey, you're Jim Henderson,
ain't you?

208
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:15,559
I heard about the murder
over at your place.

209
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,309
- Yeah.
- Hey, bartender.

210
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:21,392
Give me a beer over here
and another one for my friend.

211
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:25,349
- Heard you spent a lot of time in Nam.
- I don't talk about it.

212
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:27,670
I don't blame you,
since you spent your entire hitch

213
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:29,558
in the stockade at Fort Benning.

214
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:32,109
- How do you know that?
- Oh, I know a lot of things, Jimbo.

215
00:18:32,280 --> 00:18:33,998
Like I know
the cops was looking you over

216
00:18:34,160 --> 00:18:36,390
for the murder of Esther Scully.

217
00:18:37,160 --> 00:18:38,434
You're a cop.

218
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:41,478
Cops do not wear $500 suits,
my friend.

219
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:43,676
I talk to cops. Cops talk to me.

220
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:45,273
And do you know
what the cops told me?

221
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:47,670
That you are the only other person
with a key to the shed

222
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:50,229
where Harold Scully keeps his boots.

223
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:51,674
I was here.

224
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:53,717
- Can you prove it?
- Ask anybody.

225
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:56,997
- And where were your keys?
- With me.

226
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:00,714
They're always with me.

227
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:03,235
You know,
Harold Scully is a dear friend of mine.

228
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:06,198
Scully is a killer,
and I hope he fries.

229
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:08,112
Harold was gonna fire you,
wasn't he, Jimbo?

230
00:19:08,280 --> 00:19:09,759
He said that you
were a hopeless drunk

231
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:12,229
and he was gonna make sure
that you never got another job.

232
00:19:12,400 --> 00:19:14,550
You're asking for it.

233
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:15,709
Don't move, Jimbo.

234
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:18,635
Don't move. See, I was in Nam.

235
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:22,236
I'm the real thing.
Green Beret, black belt, Silver Star.

236
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:25,073
I was a POW and an MIA.

237
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:29,631
It was so bad where we were,
we slept in body bags just in case.

238
00:19:29,800 --> 00:19:32,997
Don't you move, Jimbo.
I'll take you out.

239
00:19:34,720 --> 00:19:37,951
By the way, here are your keys.

240
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:48,072
Listen.

241
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:52,670
- Is somebody in the house?
- I think so.

242
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:56,072
Let's find out.

243
00:20:39,560 --> 00:20:41,915
Aunt Esther's ghost?

244
00:20:52,600 --> 00:20:53,953
What are you doing here?

245
00:20:55,160 --> 00:20:57,355
Oh, I'm so sorry. Would you--?

246
00:20:57,520 --> 00:21:00,432
Oh, would you look at that?
I'm sorry.

247
00:21:00,600 --> 00:21:03,160
- I thought I was by myself.
- Who are you?

248
00:21:03,320 --> 00:21:06,118
I'm Ben Matlock, Harold's lawyer.

249
00:21:06,960 --> 00:21:10,475
- You must be his nieces.
- Yes, we are.

250
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:12,517
What gives you the right
to come in here?

251
00:21:12,680 --> 00:21:13,908
Oh, I'm so sorry to intrude.

252
00:21:14,080 --> 00:21:15,911
I have your Uncle Harold
in a hotel room.

253
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:17,069
He gave me the key,

254
00:21:17,240 --> 00:21:19,595
and I thought
you girls were at the library.

255
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:21,034
Julia?

256
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:23,156
Harold said you were the one
that did the talking,

257
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:25,038
that Emily was real shy.

258
00:21:25,200 --> 00:21:29,193
- Don't burn yourself with that.
- Oh, I'm not gonna burn anything.

259
00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:30,998
Or anyone.

260
00:21:33,960 --> 00:21:36,599
- What are you looking for?
- Silver polish.

261
00:21:36,760 --> 00:21:38,034
Why?

262
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:40,236
Whoever killed your Aunt Esther

263
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:44,154
tried to clean that candlestick
before the police found it.

264
00:21:44,320 --> 00:21:46,356
I can't find the silver polish.

265
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:50,149
If Harold tried to clean it,
where'd he put the polish?

266
00:21:50,320 --> 00:21:53,995
- How are you gonna defend Harold?
- I don't know.

267
00:21:54,160 --> 00:21:57,789
- Do you suspect Henderson?
- Well, I wouldn't rule him out.

268
00:21:58,600 --> 00:22:00,113
Look.

269
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:07,478
- Silver polish.
- Isn't that interesting?

270
00:22:08,040 --> 00:22:09,234
Because I just found this one.

271
00:22:13,160 --> 00:22:16,550
I guess you can't
have too much silver polish.

272
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:22,192
Well, I'll be on my way.
And again, I'm...

273
00:22:23,320 --> 00:22:24,469
Good night.

274
00:22:36,760 --> 00:22:38,830
- Good night.
- Good night.

275
00:22:42,520 --> 00:22:44,192
Julia?

276
00:22:52,320 --> 00:22:54,151
Julia?

277
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:05,031
Julia.

278
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:17,392
Julia!

279
00:23:17,560 --> 00:23:22,190
- I missed you by two inches.
- Is that--? Is that what it was like?

280
00:23:22,360 --> 00:23:24,635
Except for the two inches.

281
00:23:40,160 --> 00:23:44,392
I forgot to tell you.
If you need anything, just call.

282
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:48,349
Good night.

283
00:23:58,800 --> 00:24:00,791
Don't worry.

284
00:24:00,960 --> 00:24:04,236
He's smart,
but he's not smart enough.

285
00:24:11,120 --> 00:24:13,315
Good morning, Ben.
Are you ready to go to work?

286
00:24:13,480 --> 00:24:16,233
- Well, just about.
- Good.

287
00:24:16,400 --> 00:24:19,198
- Ms. Matlock.
- Mr. Burgess.

288
00:24:22,600 --> 00:24:24,955
I called you last night,
but you weren't home.

289
00:24:25,120 --> 00:24:26,678
I know.

290
00:24:30,480 --> 00:24:32,596
- Good morning, Harold.
- Morning.

291
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:38,760
Ben, I'm very nervous.

292
00:24:38,920 --> 00:24:42,390
Harold, the most important thing
you can do is sit up straight,

293
00:24:42,560 --> 00:24:44,949
look right at the jury
like you haven't done anything

294
00:24:45,120 --> 00:24:46,599
and you're not afraid of anything.

295
00:24:46,760 --> 00:24:50,196
- How long do I have to do that?
- Till I tell you to stop.

296
00:24:54,760 --> 00:24:56,352
Hi.

297
00:25:02,200 --> 00:25:04,111
Lieutenant Daniels,

298
00:25:04,280 --> 00:25:07,909
I show you People's Exhibit 5
and ask you if you recognise it.

299
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:10,116
Yes, it has my mark on it there.

300
00:25:10,280 --> 00:25:13,431
Lieutenant, blood and particle tests
have been done on this candlestick.

301
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:17,912
It's stipulated by the defence that this
is the weapon that killed Mrs. Scully.

302
00:25:21,520 --> 00:25:24,080
Please tell the court
where the candlestick was found.

303
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:26,310
Yes, it was found
wrapped in that newspaper

304
00:25:26,480 --> 00:25:29,472
in the cellar
of the defendant's home.

305
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:34,911
So it was hidden in the cellar
of Harold Scully's house?

306
00:25:35,080 --> 00:25:36,274
That is correct.

307
00:25:36,440 --> 00:25:37,998
Lieutenant, you've already testified

308
00:25:38,160 --> 00:25:40,390
that the glass door
was broken from the inside.

309
00:25:40,560 --> 00:25:43,438
That the footprints in the flower bed
were made by the defendant's boots.

310
00:25:43,600 --> 00:25:45,830
Now you tell us

311
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:48,156
that the murder weapon
was found in his home.

312
00:25:48,320 --> 00:25:52,632
Lieutenant, as an experienced
investigator, what do you conclude

313
00:25:52,800 --> 00:25:55,109
about the defendant's
claim that he saw an intruder?

314
00:25:55,280 --> 00:25:57,794
Objection, Your Honour.
Improper opinion. Relevance.

315
00:25:57,960 --> 00:25:59,473
I withdraw the question.

316
00:26:00,480 --> 00:26:01,469
There's nothing further.

317
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:03,115
Any cross?

318
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:05,794
- No, Your Honour.
- You may step down.

319
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:09,996
Your Honour, the State rests.

320
00:26:10,160 --> 00:26:11,195
Mr. Matlock?

321
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:16,437
Defence calls
James Henderson to the stand.

322
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:19,316
Mr. Henderson,
the night of the murder,

323
00:26:19,480 --> 00:26:21,994
you were in town at Archie's Tavern.
Is that correct?

324
00:26:22,160 --> 00:26:24,276
Yeah. I got witnesses.

325
00:26:24,440 --> 00:26:27,238
And before you left home,
you locked up everything?

326
00:26:27,400 --> 00:26:29,436
Sure, like always.

327
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:31,636
There are two sets of keys,
yours and Mrs. Scully's?

328
00:26:31,800 --> 00:26:33,631
- That's right.
- Okay.

329
00:26:33,800 --> 00:26:36,439
Now, the night of the murder,
how did you get home?

330
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:38,795
I got a ride from one of my buddies.

331
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:42,077
Would Mr. Robert Delaney
please stand, please?

332
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:44,232
That's the gentleman
who drove you home?

333
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:45,719
- Yeah.
- Now, Mr. Henderson,

334
00:26:45,880 --> 00:26:48,394
I want you to think very carefully.

335
00:26:48,560 --> 00:26:51,472
When you got home
the night of the murder,

336
00:26:51,640 --> 00:26:54,473
you did not open the gate, did you?

337
00:26:55,600 --> 00:26:58,273
Now, I can put Mr. Delaney
on the stand, he saw you.

338
00:26:59,280 --> 00:27:02,078
- You climbed over it, didn't you?
- Yeah.

339
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:03,434
Why'd you do that?

340
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:05,719
I didn't have the damn keys.

341
00:27:05,880 --> 00:27:09,077
Nobody else had them, either.
I found them in my apartment.

342
00:27:10,040 --> 00:27:14,158
Mr. Henderson, you just testified

343
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:15,834
that you locked everything up.

344
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,036
If your apartment was locked,
how'd you get in?

345
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:19,474
Door was open.

346
00:27:20,560 --> 00:27:24,155
So you, or someone else,
could've gone in your apartment,

347
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:26,197
gotten the keys,
and unlocked that shed.

348
00:27:26,360 --> 00:27:27,429
It wasn't me.

349
00:27:27,600 --> 00:27:30,319
But somebody could've unlocked
that shed and gotten those boots.

350
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:32,436
- Is that correct?
- That's right.

351
00:27:32,600 --> 00:27:35,273
Okay. Yeah.

352
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:36,873
Now,

353
00:27:38,560 --> 00:27:40,516
Mr. Henderson,

354
00:27:41,440 --> 00:27:42,714
you don't drive, do you?

355
00:27:42,880 --> 00:27:44,313
No.

356
00:27:44,480 --> 00:27:46,038
I lost my licence.

357
00:27:46,520 --> 00:27:48,750
Well, how did you get into town
the night of the murder?

358
00:27:49,240 --> 00:27:52,391
- I got a ride.
- From whom?

359
00:27:52,880 --> 00:27:54,233
Julia and Emily.

360
00:27:55,080 --> 00:27:56,957
Julia and Emily.

361
00:27:58,720 --> 00:28:00,278
That's very nice of them.

362
00:28:00,440 --> 00:28:03,113
Thank you. No further questions.

363
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:20,592
Good evening.

364
00:28:21,200 --> 00:28:22,519
Fine library.

365
00:28:22,680 --> 00:28:24,159
Researching the case.

366
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:26,993
<i>''lnterpretation of Perception. ''</i>

367
00:28:27,160 --> 00:28:29,515
It's good. Julia'd enjoy this.

368
00:28:32,920 --> 00:28:35,912
It's all about how sometimes
we see things that aren't there

369
00:28:36,080 --> 00:28:38,355
and other times
we don't see what is.

370
00:28:38,520 --> 00:28:39,589
Quiet.

371
00:28:40,920 --> 00:28:42,194
Sorry.

372
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:47,997
Where's Julia?
I'd like to have a word with her.

373
00:28:48,160 --> 00:28:50,196
She's here somewhere.

374
00:28:50,520 --> 00:28:54,195
Let's go ask Miss Reese.
She may have seen her.

375
00:29:05,880 --> 00:29:09,634
Mrs. Reese, have you seen Julia?

376
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:13,709
Yes, of course.
She's in the upper stacks, up there.

377
00:29:15,040 --> 00:29:18,237
There she is. Julia.

378
00:29:26,640 --> 00:29:30,235
- Where's Julia?
- Right over here.

379
00:29:34,960 --> 00:29:35,995
I could have sworn--

380
00:29:38,480 --> 00:29:41,995
Amazing how our eyes
can fool us sometimes.

381
00:29:42,480 --> 00:29:44,516
Mr. Hudson
was interviewing your sister.

382
00:29:44,680 --> 00:29:48,275
He tricked me. I thought it was you.

383
00:29:48,440 --> 00:29:50,476
So did Mrs. Reese.

384
00:29:50,640 --> 00:29:52,039
I don't think she's so certain now

385
00:29:52,200 --> 00:29:55,317
it was you she saw in the stacks
the night of the murder.

386
00:29:56,880 --> 00:29:58,791
We have to study.

387
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:00,871
One other thing.

388
00:30:01,480 --> 00:30:03,869
Subpoenas for both of you.

389
00:30:04,280 --> 00:30:07,955
You can come to court together.
It'll be like always.

390
00:30:15,240 --> 00:30:16,719
- Well, that's it.
- Guess so.

391
00:30:16,880 --> 00:30:18,871
- Take you to dinner?
- No thanks, Dad.

392
00:30:20,160 --> 00:30:22,799
You're not trying to ask me where
I'm spending my evening, are you?

393
00:30:22,960 --> 00:30:25,155
I respect your privacy.

394
00:30:25,320 --> 00:30:26,469
Drop you off somewhere?

395
00:30:27,520 --> 00:30:28,589
Good night.

396
00:30:34,720 --> 00:30:37,109
Tyler, do you still have the keys
to the Scully house?

397
00:30:37,280 --> 00:30:38,872
Now, why do you wanna go there?

398
00:30:39,040 --> 00:30:40,712
Harold asked me
to pick up a few things.

399
00:30:40,880 --> 00:30:44,350
Some soap that doesn't dry his skin,
some cologne that doesn't burn him,

400
00:30:44,520 --> 00:30:46,909
little bedroom slippers,
and pyjamas with the horsies on them.

401
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:50,755
I didn't tell Dad because, well,
you know how he feels about Harold.

402
00:30:51,240 --> 00:30:53,071
And you want to go alone?

403
00:30:53,240 --> 00:30:56,710
Yeah. I mean, nobody's there,
and the girls are still studying.

404
00:30:58,760 --> 00:31:00,591
You got it.

405
00:31:21,960 --> 00:31:23,837
Horsies.

406
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:46,598
Is anyone there?

407
00:32:02,280 --> 00:32:03,269
Julia?

408
00:32:05,200 --> 00:32:06,758
Emily?

409
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:08,877
Help.

410
00:32:29,160 --> 00:32:31,674
Help me.

411
00:32:31,840 --> 00:32:33,398
Who's there?

412
00:32:55,720 --> 00:32:57,517
Anybody here?

413
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:05,437
Open the door.

414
00:33:41,120 --> 00:33:44,430
- Charlene?
- Dad?

415
00:33:45,120 --> 00:33:46,997
Dad, I'm down here.

416
00:33:47,720 --> 00:33:49,039
- Charlene.
- Dad.

417
00:33:49,200 --> 00:33:51,395
- You all right?
- Yes.

418
00:33:59,800 --> 00:34:01,950
I could've broken my neck.

419
00:34:03,760 --> 00:34:05,432
We didn't know you were here.

420
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:07,955
We were just playing a game.

421
00:34:08,120 --> 00:34:10,190
I'm sure you were.

422
00:34:20,800 --> 00:34:22,631
Harold told me you were here.
I got worried.

423
00:34:22,800 --> 00:34:25,872
- They did it, Dad.
- I know.

424
00:34:47,400 --> 00:34:48,549
Morning, Julia.

425
00:34:49,480 --> 00:34:50,799
What are you doing here?

426
00:34:50,960 --> 00:34:53,030
I think we should talk.

427
00:34:53,680 --> 00:34:55,159
Let you in on a secret.

428
00:34:56,000 --> 00:34:59,276
I put a little cream cheese in the eggs.
Makes them richer.

429
00:34:59,840 --> 00:35:01,637
There's toast. I couldn't find jelly.

430
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:03,153
Third cabinet, second shelf.

431
00:35:05,760 --> 00:35:08,069
I gotta level with you, Julia.

432
00:35:08,240 --> 00:35:10,879
I didn't wanna represent Harold,

433
00:35:11,080 --> 00:35:12,638
but I'm really glad I am.

434
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:15,439
I never had a case like this one.

435
00:35:16,160 --> 00:35:18,674
- Why is that?
- No motive.

436
00:35:19,520 --> 00:35:21,351
Harold hated Esther.

437
00:35:21,960 --> 00:35:23,029
Harold didn't kill Esther.

438
00:35:23,640 --> 00:35:25,596
And you think you know who did?

439
00:35:25,760 --> 00:35:27,910
Let's just say maybe I do.

440
00:35:28,080 --> 00:35:30,275
But I still can't figure out...

441
00:35:30,440 --> 00:35:32,032
No motive.

442
00:35:38,920 --> 00:35:39,955
Emily!

443
00:35:40,120 --> 00:35:42,793
You'll destroy your life
if you go along with Julia.

444
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:44,632
I don't know what to do.

445
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:49,157
Come with me, please,
before it's too late.

446
00:35:50,960 --> 00:35:52,632
Leave her alone!

447
00:35:53,120 --> 00:35:54,951
Are you all right?

448
00:35:59,600 --> 00:36:01,556
Get out of here.

449
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:08,151
Both of you.

450
00:36:09,400 --> 00:36:10,879
Let's go, Harold.

451
00:36:24,800 --> 00:36:26,028
It worked, Ben.

452
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:28,475
Julia's hold on her's too strong.

453
00:36:28,640 --> 00:36:31,313
What are we gonna do, Ben?
Ben, what are we gonna do?

454
00:36:31,480 --> 00:36:34,950
Harold, we're both gonna fall down.

455
00:36:36,040 --> 00:36:37,314
Ben, what are we gonna do?

456
00:36:37,480 --> 00:36:39,835
- I'll get to the bottom of it.
- I hope.

457
00:36:41,480 --> 00:36:42,515
Emily,

458
00:36:42,680 --> 00:36:46,150
where were you Wednesday night,
the night of the murder?

459
00:36:46,320 --> 00:36:48,151
- I was at the library.
- With your sister?

460
00:36:48,320 --> 00:36:49,799
That's right.

461
00:36:49,960 --> 00:36:52,918
But don't you usually have
bridge club on Wednesday night?

462
00:36:53,080 --> 00:36:55,230
- Yes.
- I bet you're pretty good.

463
00:36:55,400 --> 00:36:56,549
Yes, I am.

464
00:36:56,720 --> 00:36:58,870
In fact, you haven't missed
a Wednesday of bridge

465
00:36:59,040 --> 00:37:00,314
in over a year, have you?

466
00:37:00,840 --> 00:37:01,875
No.

467
00:37:02,040 --> 00:37:05,191
But the night of the murder, you
didn't go to your bridge party, did you?

468
00:37:05,840 --> 00:37:06,829
No.

469
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:09,116
Objection. Relevancy.

470
00:37:09,720 --> 00:37:11,915
Your Honour, we're looking toward
developing facts

471
00:37:12,080 --> 00:37:15,993
to indicate another person, or persons',
commission of this terrible crime.

472
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:19,872
Very well, but try to move it along.

473
00:37:20,040 --> 00:37:21,678
Now, Emily,

474
00:37:21,960 --> 00:37:24,520
why didn't you play bridge
that Wednesday night?

475
00:37:25,880 --> 00:37:28,030
I told you, I was in the library.

476
00:37:31,280 --> 00:37:34,113
Must've been something
very important.

477
00:37:35,040 --> 00:37:36,359
- An exam?
- No.

478
00:37:36,520 --> 00:37:38,556
- A paper?
- Yes.

479
00:37:38,720 --> 00:37:40,039
On what subject?

480
00:37:40,200 --> 00:37:42,668
It was for my Econometric seminar.

481
00:37:44,800 --> 00:37:48,190
If I had spoken to Mr. Harvey Gleason,
your professor,

482
00:37:48,360 --> 00:37:50,715
at his office over at Campbell Hall,

483
00:37:50,880 --> 00:37:56,238
I would bet that he wouldn't have been
able to recall any such assignment.

484
00:37:56,720 --> 00:37:58,119
You're making me nervous.

485
00:37:58,280 --> 00:38:00,999
Oh, take your time, take your time.

486
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:05,278
- It was for Russian Lit.
- No, it wasn't.

487
00:38:05,440 --> 00:38:06,919
And it wasn't for Statistics,

488
00:38:07,080 --> 00:38:10,675
or Economic History,
or Advanced Monetary Theory, either.

489
00:38:11,480 --> 00:38:13,072
You didn't have any papers.

490
00:38:13,240 --> 00:38:14,912
You didn't have any tests.

491
00:38:15,080 --> 00:38:18,755
Isn't it true you didn't play bridge

492
00:38:19,960 --> 00:38:23,316
because Julia made you
go to the library?

493
00:38:23,480 --> 00:38:24,959
No.

494
00:38:25,120 --> 00:38:27,839
And didn't you go there
to give your sister an alibi?

495
00:38:28,280 --> 00:38:30,032
No.

496
00:38:37,680 --> 00:38:39,318
Emily,

497
00:38:41,040 --> 00:38:43,076
when you were very young,

498
00:38:43,240 --> 00:38:45,356
your parents died in a fire,
didn't they?

499
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:47,912
Yes.

500
00:38:48,080 --> 00:38:50,435
And you always felt bad about that,
didn't you?

501
00:38:50,600 --> 00:38:52,670
Even guilty?

502
00:38:52,840 --> 00:38:54,558
Yes.

503
00:38:55,360 --> 00:38:56,793
Yes.

504
00:39:01,120 --> 00:39:04,510
Did you know that fire
was caused by a broken gas main?

505
00:39:06,280 --> 00:39:07,918
No.

506
00:39:08,080 --> 00:39:10,674
I have the fire-department
report right here.

507
00:39:11,280 --> 00:39:13,953
Julia didn't tell you that, did she?

508
00:39:17,360 --> 00:39:20,193
She needed for you
to feel responsible

509
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:22,669
for the fire
and the death of your parents

510
00:39:23,320 --> 00:39:26,437
because you used to like
to play with matches.

511
00:39:30,400 --> 00:39:31,833
Emily,

512
00:39:32,280 --> 00:39:35,317
if you'll help me, if you'll be strong,

513
00:39:36,160 --> 00:39:39,994
you can end the domination
of your sister over you right now.

514
00:39:40,160 --> 00:39:41,593
Leave my sister alone.

515
00:39:41,760 --> 00:39:43,637
Why are you letting him
attack her like this?

516
00:39:43,800 --> 00:39:47,236
Can't you see what he's doing to her?
He's a liar. Liar!

517
00:39:47,400 --> 00:39:48,594
Order. Order in the court.

518
00:39:48,760 --> 00:39:52,594
One more word out of you, young lady,
and I'll have you in contempt.

519
00:40:03,520 --> 00:40:05,238
No further questions.

520
00:40:05,720 --> 00:40:08,393
I have no questions
of this witness, Your Honour.

521
00:40:08,560 --> 00:40:09,913
You may step down.

522
00:40:28,520 --> 00:40:30,078
Julia,

523
00:40:33,200 --> 00:40:34,997
the court isn't finished with you.

524
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:39,836
Your Honour, I call Julia
McCullough to the stand.

525
00:40:42,480 --> 00:40:46,632
Now, Julia, you're a very smart
young woman, aren't you?

526
00:40:46,800 --> 00:40:48,028
No, not really.

527
00:40:48,200 --> 00:40:49,838
I've got your records right here.

528
00:40:50,000 --> 00:40:53,709
You graduated college at 1 9,
already have your Master's degree.

529
00:40:53,880 --> 00:40:56,713
Working on your Ph.D.
in Clinical Psychology, aren't you?

530
00:40:56,880 --> 00:40:57,949
Yes.

531
00:40:58,120 --> 00:41:01,237
Your Master's thesis was on
resulting criminal behaviour

532
00:41:01,400 --> 00:41:05,109
in the emotionally deprived
and intellectually gifted adolescent.

533
00:41:06,160 --> 00:41:08,469
You can almost be an expert witness
in this case.

534
00:41:08,640 --> 00:41:10,153
I don't think so.

535
00:41:10,320 --> 00:41:12,356
Maybe you could help me out.

536
00:41:12,760 --> 00:41:13,749
Based on your research,

537
00:41:13,920 --> 00:41:17,276
what sort of person
commits a motiveless crime?

538
00:41:17,440 --> 00:41:20,079
Actually, there have been some
case studies on that subject.

539
00:41:20,240 --> 00:41:24,756
Well, does that sort of crime,
in this case, murder, have a name?

540
00:41:25,960 --> 00:41:27,393
It's called a sadistic murder.

541
00:41:27,560 --> 00:41:30,472
How do your studies
describe such a killer?

542
00:41:30,640 --> 00:41:33,996
It's usually someone
who suffered emotional trauma

543
00:41:34,160 --> 00:41:36,230
followed by long emotional isolation.

544
00:41:37,240 --> 00:41:39,708
If we were to meet such a person,

545
00:41:39,880 --> 00:41:42,075
how would they appear
on the surface?

546
00:41:42,240 --> 00:41:43,389
Very self-reliant.

547
00:41:43,560 --> 00:41:45,357
Do they usually show

548
00:41:46,120 --> 00:41:48,429
lack of emotion, sometimes total?

549
00:41:48,600 --> 00:41:49,919
Sometimes, yes.

550
00:41:50,080 --> 00:41:52,071
- Is there a purpose to the crime?
- Revenge.

551
00:41:52,240 --> 00:41:53,229
Against whom?

552
00:41:55,520 --> 00:41:57,397
They're rebelling against the world.

553
00:41:57,560 --> 00:41:59,676
They find it cruel and unfeeling.

554
00:42:01,320 --> 00:42:04,835
Do they think they're invulnerable?

555
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:06,669
Some of them.

556
00:42:06,840 --> 00:42:08,956
- Superior?
- Yes.

557
00:42:09,120 --> 00:42:12,271
Do you consider Harold Scully
to be a superior person?

558
00:42:17,000 --> 00:42:18,558
That's not for me to say.

559
00:42:18,720 --> 00:42:22,429
If he were, probably no one would be
able to prove he committed this crime.

560
00:42:22,600 --> 00:42:23,874
I wouldn't know.

561
00:42:25,640 --> 00:42:28,154
If you committed this crime,

562
00:42:28,520 --> 00:42:30,033
would anyone be able to prove it?

563
00:42:30,200 --> 00:42:33,078
Objection. Calls for speculation.

564
00:42:33,240 --> 00:42:34,992
Sustained.

565
00:42:35,160 --> 00:42:38,038
Do you consider yourself superior?

566
00:42:38,400 --> 00:42:39,389
No.

567
00:42:40,240 --> 00:42:41,912
Now, Julia,

568
00:42:42,680 --> 00:42:44,636
you stole Mr. Henderson's keys

569
00:42:44,800 --> 00:42:46,836
the night you drove him into town,
didn't you?

570
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:48,797
- No.
- Then made your sister

571
00:42:48,960 --> 00:42:50,837
impersonate you in the library.

572
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:52,672
- No.
- Then you killed your Aunt Esther

573
00:42:52,840 --> 00:42:54,512
to prove you could commit
the perfect crime

574
00:42:54,680 --> 00:42:56,716
and were therefore
better than everyone else.

575
00:42:56,880 --> 00:42:58,108
- No.
- Then you and your sister

576
00:42:58,280 --> 00:43:00,669
hid this candlestick
in the basement for the police to find.

577
00:43:00,840 --> 00:43:03,354
- Of course not.
- Who did?

578
00:43:05,120 --> 00:43:08,237
- Uncle Harold.
- I don't think so.

579
00:43:09,280 --> 00:43:15,799
I just noticed something that somehow
evaded me until a moment ago.

580
00:43:17,800 --> 00:43:19,836
If it had been a snake,
it would've bitten me.

581
00:43:21,600 --> 00:43:24,319
When does the paper arrive
at the house?

582
00:43:24,480 --> 00:43:26,232
- In the morning.
- What time?

583
00:43:27,320 --> 00:43:28,639
About 6:00.

584
00:43:29,160 --> 00:43:32,948
Well, Harold couldn't have used this
newspaper to wrap the candlestick.

585
00:43:33,120 --> 00:43:34,348
Why not?

586
00:43:37,200 --> 00:43:38,519
Read the day of the week.

587
00:43:39,560 --> 00:43:41,312
Read it.

588
00:43:45,240 --> 00:43:48,118
- It's Thursday's paper.
- I didn't hear you.

589
00:43:49,120 --> 00:43:51,031
It's Thursday's paper.

590
00:43:51,200 --> 00:43:53,953
And Uncle Harold
was taken into custody by the police

591
00:43:54,120 --> 00:43:56,509
Wednesday night, wasn't he?

592
00:44:01,440 --> 00:44:03,749
- Yes.
- So, what does that mean?

593
00:44:04,520 --> 00:44:07,717
It means this candlestick
was placed in the basement

594
00:44:07,880 --> 00:44:10,838
after Harold was taken
to police headquarters.

595
00:44:12,200 --> 00:44:14,998
Who was at the house
Thursday morning, Julia?

596
00:44:15,600 --> 00:44:17,636
The police were with you.

597
00:44:17,880 --> 00:44:20,474
They know that you and Emily
were alone.

598
00:44:20,640 --> 00:44:21,834
Only you and your sister

599
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:24,912
could've placed this candlestick
in the basement.

600
00:44:55,320 --> 00:44:57,550
Your Honour, in fairness
and in the interest of justice,

601
00:44:57,720 --> 00:44:59,153
State moves for dismissal.

602
00:44:59,320 --> 00:45:01,880
Motion is granted.
This court is adjourned.

603
00:45:04,360 --> 00:45:06,510
- Congratulations, Harold.
- I'm so relieved.

604
00:45:06,680 --> 00:45:07,954
Isn't he great?

605
00:45:13,120 --> 00:45:16,192
Oh, Ben. Ben, thank you, thank you.

606
00:45:16,360 --> 00:45:17,588
My very own nieces.

607
00:45:17,760 --> 00:45:19,432
- Thank you, Ben, thank you.
- Ben. Ben.

608
00:45:19,600 --> 00:45:21,079
- Congratulations.
- Thank you.

609
00:45:21,240 --> 00:45:23,595
Charlene, Dale and Stephanie
will meet us at the restaurant.

610
00:45:23,760 --> 00:45:24,749
I'll see you there.

611
00:45:28,000 --> 00:45:30,036
I'll see you tomorrow, Dad. Harold.

612
00:45:30,200 --> 00:45:33,158
- Oh, Charlene, thank you.
- You're very welcome.

613
00:45:33,320 --> 00:45:35,470
Oh, Ben, Ben, thank you, thank you.

614
00:45:35,640 --> 00:45:37,631
And we'll meet
at the club tomorrow for golf.

615
00:45:37,800 --> 00:45:38,915
- No golf.
- How about lunch?

616
00:45:39,080 --> 00:45:40,752
Actually, I don't eat on Tuesdays.

617
00:45:40,920 --> 00:45:43,753
Actually, Tyler and I
have a case in Philadelphia.

618
00:45:43,920 --> 00:45:46,673
You've got a case in Philadelphia.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
